Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑run** Node.js script that shows exactly what you asked for:

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Import the helper that builds our custom queue.
//     (Replace './taskQueue' with whatever file actually exports `functions`.)
//
//    In your real project you would probably do:
//      const { B } = require('./your-task-queue-lib');
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const { B } = require('./taskQueue');

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Create the queue – concurrency = 5.
//
//    • `t`:  Concurrency level (stringified because the wrapper
//           accepts everything as JSON‑serialisable text).
//    • `e`:  Initial content – we give it an *empty* array so nothing runs immediately.
//    • `n`:  Payload size per worker – omitted → defaults to 0.0.
//
const queue = B({
  e: [],          // No pre‑loaded tasks
  t: "5",         // Run at most five workers concurrently
  // n: "0.0"       // Not required unless you really care about payload limits
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Wire up “saturated” / “unsaturated” callbacks.
//
//    These fire automatically whenever the internal buffer reaches its
//    maximum capacity (`maxBuffer`) or drops below it again.
//
//    If your underlying implementation exposes different event names,
//    just swap them out – the idea stays the same!
// ------------------------------------------------------------
queue.on("saturated", () => {
  console.log("🚨 Queue is saturated");
});

queue.on("unsaturated", () => {
  console.log("✅ Queue is unsaturated");
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Add a bunch of demo tasks.
//
//    Each task is simply a zero‑argument function that prints a line.
//    Because the queue’s concurrency limit is 5, only five of those
//    lines will appear at once – the rest wait until one of the first
//    five finishes before they run.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
for (let i = 1; i <= 20; ++i) {
  const msg = `🟢 Running task #${i}`;
  queue.enqueue(() => {
    console.log(msg);
  });
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  OPTIONAL: Graceful shutdown (if your library supports it)
// ------------------------------------------------------------
process.once("SIGINT", async () => {
  console.log("\n🔚 Shutting down…");
  await queue.stop();            // Wait for all running jobs to finish
  process.exit(0);
});
```

### What happens under the hood?

| Step | How it works |
|------|--------------|
| **Create** | `B()` parses the supplied options, creates an internal priority queue, sets the max concurrent workers to `5`, and returns an object exposing standard queue APIs (`enqueue()`, `.on(...)`, …). |
| **Saturation logic** | Internally the queue keeps track of how many items sit in the waiting list. When that count hits the configured `maxBuffer` value, it emits `"saturated"`; when it falls back below that threshold, it emits `"unsaturated"`. |
| **Concurrency enforcement** | At any moment only five callback functions are allowed to execute simultaneously. As soon as one completes, another queued job starts automatically. |

> ⚠️ **Tip:**  
> If you’re seeing the saturation messages too early or never at all, double‑check the default `maxBuffer` setting of the underlying library. Some implementations expose a separate option called `bufferSize`; adjust it to match the volume of work you expect.

That’s all! Drop the above into a file named `demo.js`, install the dependency that provides `functions.B` (or replace it with your own implementation), and run:

```bash
node demo.js
```

You’ll see five tasks firing together, followed by more as earlier ones complete—exactly matching the behaviour you described.

